Shiny provides `isolate()` to resolve the problem of undesired reactive dependency. This function allows you to access the current value of a reactive value or expression without taking a dependency on it:

:::::{.my-r-code}
:::{.my-r-code-header}
:::::: {#cnj-15-using-isolate}
: Accessing reactive value without taking a dependency on it
::::::
:::
::::{.my-r-code-container}
::: {#lst-15-using-isolate}

::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
r <- reactiveValues(count = 0, x = 1)
r$count 
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
#> [1] 0
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
class(r)
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
#> [1] "reactivevalues"
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
observe({
  r$x
  r$count <- isolate(r$count) + 1
})

r$x <- 1
r$x <- 2
r$count
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
#> [1] 0
```


:::

```{.r .cell-code}
r$x <- 3
r$count
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}

```
#> [1] 0
```


:::
:::


Using `isolate()`: Accessing reactive values or reactive expressions without taking a dependency on it
:::

::::
:::::

Like `observe()`, a lot of the time you don’t need to use `isolate()` directly because there are two useful functions that wrap up the most common usage: `observeEvent()` and `eventReactive()`.


### `observeEvent()` and `eventReactive()`

When you saw the code above, you might have remembered @sec-03-observers and wondered why we didn’t use `observeEvent()`:

:::::{.my-r-code}
:::{.my-r-code-header}
:::::: {#cnj-15-replace-observe-with-observeEvent}
: Replace `observe()` with `observeEvent()`
::::::
:::
::::{.my-r-code-container}
::: {#lst-15-replace-observe-with-observeEvent}


::: {.cell}

```{.r .cell-code}
observeEvent(r$x, { 
  r$count <- r$count + 1 
}) 
```
:::


To prevent an infinite loop with `observe()` in @lst-15-infinite-loop-observer you could use `isolate()` as in @lst-15-using-isolate, but also  `observeEvent()`
:::

::::
:::::




Indeed, we could used `observeEvent(x, y)` because it is equivalent to` observe({x; isolate(y)})`. It elegantly decouples what you want to listen to from what action you want to take. And `eventReactive()` performs the analogous job for reactives: `eventReactive(x, y)` is equivalent to `reactive({x; isolate(y)})`.

`observeEvent()` and `eventReactive()` have additional arguments that allow you to control the details of their operation:

- By default, both functions will ignore any event that yields NULL (or in the special case of action buttons, 0). Use `ignoreNULL = FALSE` to also handle NULL values.
- By default both functions will run once when you create them. Use `ignoreInit = TRUE` to skip this run.
- For `observeEvent()` only, you can use `once = TRUE` to run the handler only once.


